  "hints": [
    {
      "tier": 1,
      "text": "Ignore the pictures for a moment and just look at the sign numbers. Some numbers show up in several different words (12, 30, 10, 3, 9). Treat every number as a little meaning-piece that can be reused, and start by grouping the words that share a piece.",
      "xpCost": 2
    },
    {
      "tier": 2,
      "text": "Most words are compounds: a modifying sign followed by a general sign, so the last sign often names the general category (a kind of building, a kind of person, a kind of food) and the earlier sign narrows it down. So group the English glosses by category too, and match a group of words that share a final sign to the group of glosses that share a category. Also count: a gloss that packs two ideas in English usually needs two signs.",
      "xpCost": 3
    },
    {
      "tier": 3,
      "text": "Look for words that literally contain other words. (O) is 18-8-9 and (T) is 8-9 — the second is inside the first with one extra sign in front. Whatever (T) means, (O) should mean that thing modified by sign 18. The same nesting happens with (U) 15-7 inside (I) 15-7-3, and with (W) 13-30 inside (A) 13-30-4. Each nesting pins down one extra sign's meaning, and the leftover single sign is exactly what part G2 asks you to name.",
      "xpCost": 4
    }
  ],
  "explanationHtml": "<h3>What kind of language is this?</h3><p>Cistercian Sign Language has only a few dozen basic signs, so it builds bigger meanings by stringing signs together into compounds - the way English makes <em>doghouse</em> out of <em>dog</em> and <em>house</em>. That means the puzzle is really about morphology (how words are built out of smaller meaning-pieces, called morphemes): each number is one morpheme, and each lettered group is one compound word.</p>\n<h3>Step 1: count the pieces</h3><p>Compare how many signs a word has with how many ideas its English gloss needs. \"Milk\" and \"(to) drink\" and \"apple\" are single simple ideas, and there are exactly three one-sign words in the data: (F) 1, (G) 6, (J) 17. \"A Cistercian monk\" and \"queen bee\" are complicated, and sure enough the longest words have three or four signs.</p>\n<h3>Step 2: find the repeated signs</h3><p>Three words end in sign 12: 26-12, 28-12, 27-12. Three glosses name rooms or buildings: barn, bathroom, dormitory. So sign 12 must mean roughly \"house/room\", and the first sign says which kind - a bathroom is a \"shame-house\", a dormitory a \"sleep-house\", a barn a \"cow-house\". Deciding which is which is where the photographs matter: the sign for shame covers the face, the sign for sleep rests the head on the hands, and the cow sign puts two fingers up at the temples like horns.</p><p>Sign 30 works the same way: it appears in 13-30, 5-30, 13-30-4 and 21-30-4, and four glosses talk about members of religious orders. Sign 30 is \"monk\". Now use the drawings on the last page: Cistercians wear white, Benedictines wear black, so 13 = white and 5 = black. That gives \"white-monk\" for the Cistercians as a group and \"black-monk\" for the Benedictines. Adding sign 4 (the raised index finger, \"one person\") turns the group into a single individual, and swapping 13 for 21 (\"female\") gives the word for a nun.</p>\n<h3>Step 3: chain the discoveries</h3><ul><li>13 = white, so 13-14 (\"white-\" plus something falling) is snow, and 14 by itself means rain.</li><li>5 = black, so 5-17 is \"black-milk\" - chocolate milk - and the lone sign 17 is milk.</li><li>15-7 is \"hard-water\" = ice; add sign 3 and you get Iceland, so 3 marks a country or land. That makes 1-2-3 a country whose stereotype is drinking something (\"drink-tea-land\") and 16-8-16-3 a country described by a tall shape between two identical signs.</li><li>10 = bread: 19-10 is \"God-bread\" and 20-10 is \"sweet-bread\". A ritual bread versus a cake - and 19 = God also turns up in 23-19-24, \"baby-God-day\", the day of God's baby, i.e. Christmas.</li><li>8 = tall and 9 = wood, so 8-9 = tree and 9-31 = a table made of wood. Put sign 18 in front of the tree and you get 18-8-9, a pun on \"pour-tree\" for poetry.</li><li>21-22-20-29 is \"female-king-sweet-wing\": a sweet-making winged female ruler, a queen bee.</li></ul>\n<h3>Step 4: reading off part G2</h3><p>Once each compound is decomposed, the single signs asked for in G2 simply fall out of the leftovers. \"Baby\" is the first sign of the Christmas word, \"(to) pour\" is the sign added to \"tree\" to make poetry, \"rain\" is the second sign of \"snow\", and \"tea\" is the middle sign of the country described as tea-drinking. This is the neatest lesson of the problem: you can learn the meaning of a sign you have never been shown on its own, purely from the compounds it appears in.</p>",
  "reviewStatus": "drafted",
